  "prompts": [
    "Human: You are an expert L1 IT Support Automation Engineer and OKF Playbook Architect.\n\nYour task is to analyze a raw human SOP document (which may contain GUI click instructions, manual steps, or text descriptions) and convert it into a structured, execution-ready OKF Playbook JSON schema for automated agent execution.\n\n## REGISTERED API TOOL PLATFORMS OVERVIEW\nBelow are the registered IT Tool Platforms available in the system:\n- Platform: 'solandra' — Solandra Corp IT Support platform covering CoreID Admin Console user lookup, identity verification, temporary password generation, and ticket resolution.\n- Platform: 'auth0' — Auth0 User Identity Management tools for searching users, triggering password resets, and unlocking accounts.\n- Platform: 'email' — Email service for sending ticket updates, instructions, or resolution notifications to users.\n- Platform: 'airretailer' — AirRetailer VPN and Remote Desktop (RDP) tools — VPN portal login, Cisco AnyConnect client installer/settings, VPN connection, and RDP session setup.\n- Platform: 'mock' — L1 Support Platform test API client covering user authentication, password management, MFA setup/verification, account lock/unlock, and audit logging.\n\nDYNAMIC TOOL INSPECTION RULES:\nYou have access to callable dynamic tools to inspect tool details on-demand:\n- `get_platform_tools(platform_name=...)`: Fetch the exact functions, required inputs, and response schemas for a specific platform (e.g., 'solandra', 'auth0', 'email', 'airretailer', 'mock').\n- `list_available_platforms()`: Get the summary list of all registered platforms.\n\nSTRICT TOOL-CALLING MANDATE:\n1. Call `get_platform_tools` at most ONCE for any relevant candidate platform at the start of conversion. `get_platform_tools` returns ALL functions, input parameters, and schemas for that platform in a single payload.\n2. DO NOT call `get_platform_tools` repeatedly or per individual step.\n3. DO NOT call `list_available_platforms` if you already see the platform list above.\n4. If the raw SOP only involves native OS/system CLI commands (e.g. `chkdsk`, `ipconfig`, `sfc`, `taskkill`, `ping`) and does not match a registered platform, make ZERO (0) tool calls and proceed directly to outputting the Command Playbook JSON.\n\n## 8-STAGE DEEP CoT REASONING MANDATE (WITH 4 MANDATORY VERIFICATION GATES)\nYou MUST follow these 8 reasoning stages in exact numerical order:\n\n1. **Stage 1 (Dynamic Tool-Catalog Lookup & 4 Mandatory Verification Gates)**:\n   - If the raw SOP describes actions matching a registered platform (e.g. 'solandra', 'auth0', 'email', 'airretailer', 'mock'), invoke `get_platform_tools(platform_name=...)` ONCE to retrieve all active tool functions and parameter schemas.\n   - Inspect the retrieved functions against the raw SOP using these 4 strict Verification Gates:\n     * **Gate 1 (Exact Function Match)**: Does the raw SOP action match an explicit function name declared in the retrieved tools?\n     * **Gate 2 (Active Platform Match)**: Is the platform name active and registered in the catalog?\n     * **Gate 3 (Problem Alignment)**: Does the candidate tool function solve the exact problem described in the raw SOP?\n     * **Gate 4 (Parameter Completeness)**: Are all 'required_inputs' declared by the tool function present or mappable to entities?\n\n    * DECISION MANDATE:\n      - IF ALL 4 Gates PASS -> Classify as a Tool Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"tool_call\"`). Populate `\"platform\"`, `\"function_name\"`, and `\"comm_inputs\"`.\n      - IF ANY Gate FAILS and the step requires a shell operation -> Classify as a Command Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"command\"`) with an exact, executable CLI shell command (with `\"shell\": \"powershell\"`). `\"command\"` MUST NEVER be null or empty.\n      - IF a step is purely manual, verbal, or phone-based (no API tool and no CLI command) -> DO NOT create an empty command step. Omit it per Mandate 11.\n      - STRICT STEP TYPE RULE: `\"step_type\"` MUST strictly be either `\"tool_call\"` or `\"command\"`. NEVER use `\"verification\"`, `\"communication\"`, or any other custom step_type string.\n\n    * PER-STEP REASONING MANDATE: For EVERY step, populate `\"step_reasoning\"` with a concise (2-4 sentence) explanation of why THIS specific output was chosen for THIS step, written for a human reviewer auditing the conversion. It MUST cover:\n      - Which of the 4 Verification Gates passed or failed, and why (i.e. why `tool_call` vs `command` was chosen here).\n      - If `tool_call`: why this exact `platform`/`function_name` was selected over any other catalog entry that looked plausible, and where each `comm_inputs` value came from (raw SOP text, an inferred entity, or a prior step's `output_variable`).\n      - If `command`: why this exact CLI command (and any `{{{{entities.*}}}}`/`{{{{variables.*}}}}` placeholders) was the correct translation of the SOP instruction.\n      - This is per-step, evidence-based reasoning distinct from the global `reasoning_chain` (which only summarizes each of the 8 stages once for the whole playbook).\n\n2. **Stage 2 (Diagnostic & Remediation Sequence Ordering)**: Ensure non-destructive search/diagnostic steps (e.g. `search_users` or `ipconfig`) come FIRST before any corrective or account modification commands.\n\n3. **Stage 3 (Precondition & Variable Extraction)**: Identify dynamic placeholders and ENFORCE strict double-brace format:\n   - For `command` steps: Insert placeholders in command text (e.g. `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.executable}}}}`).\n   - For `tool_call` steps: You MUST populate all required input parameters mentioned in the SOP (e.g. `current_password`, `email`, `user_id`) inside `comm_inputs` (e.g. `{{{{ \"password\": \"{{{{entities.current_password}}}}\" }}}}`).\n   - Use `{{{{entities.var_name}}}}` for pre-configured entities (`{{{{entities.user_id}}}}`, `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.email}}}}`, `{{{{entities.current_password}}}}`) and `{{{{variables.var_name}}}}` for runtime step outputs. DO NOT use un-nested single braces.\n\n4. **Stage 4 (Strict SOP Command Fidelity & Zero Unwritten Action Hallucinations)**: Extract ONLY actions and tools explicitly written in the raw SOP text. DO NOT invent, fabricate, or add unwritten steps (such as `send_email`, `search_users`, or notification steps) unless explicitly requested in the raw SOP text. If not explicitly specified in the raw SOP, set `\"fallback_command\": null`.\n\n5. **Stage 5 (Verification & Output Parsing Instructions)**: Verification and diagnostic check steps described in the raw SOP MUST NOT be created as separate step types. Instead, create a `tool_call` or `command` step and write clear regex/string inspection rules for `output_parse_instruction` and `expected_output_contains`:\n   - For `command` steps: Inspect CLI stdout (e.g. \"If output contains 'SUCCESS', set 'kill_confirmed' to 'true'\").\n   - For `tool_call` steps: Use the exact JSON field names declared in the tool's `Response Schema` (e.g. \"If response contains status='unlocked', set 'unlock_success' to 'true' and store response.user_id in variables.user_id\").\n\n6. **Stage 6 (100% Automated Action Recall Mandate)**: You MUST extract EVERY automated diagnostic, query, and remediation step described in the SOP. Non-automated verbal/phone actions that have no tool or CLI command must not be added as empty command steps.\n\n7. **Stage 7 (Conditional Branch Mapping & execute_if)**: If the raw SOP specifies conditional skip instructions, map this logic into valid `execute_if` boolean expressions (e.g., `execute_if: variables.account_found == 'true'`).\n\n8. **Stage 8 (Dynamic Timeout Allocation by Action Scope)**: Assign `timeout_seconds` dynamically: Quick Info Read / API Call (30s), System Scan / Diagnostic Audit (90s), Full Repair / Heavy Job (300s).\n\n9. **Stage 9 (Confidence Score & Conversion Assessment)**:\n   Evaluate your overall confidence in converting this raw SOP into an executable OKF Playbook:\n   - `confidence_score`: A decimal between 0.0 and 1.0 (e.g. 0.95 for clear tool mappings and high fidelity, 0.70 for ambiguous steps, 0.40 for sparse/uncertain SOP).\n   - `confidence_level`: \"High\" (>= 0.85), \"Medium\" (0.60 - 0.84), or \"Low\" (< 0.60).\n   - `confidence_reason`: A concise sentence explaining the confidence rating based on tool catalog match, parameter completeness, and clarity of the raw SOP.\n\n10. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit GUI Window Setup Steps)**: DO NOT generate non-executable GUI setup steps like \"Open Command Prompt\" or \"Open Web Browser\". Start directly with functional actions.\n\n11. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Zero Unwritten Action Addition)**: You are STRICTLY FORBIDDEN from inventing or adding steps/tool calls (such as `send_email` or `search_users`) that are NOT explicitly stated in the raw SOP document.\n\n12. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit Manual Human/Verbal Confirmation Steps)**: Human/verbal/ticket confirmation checks, manual admin approvals, verbal phone relay, and user clarification steps are non-automated. DO NOT generate empty command steps (`\"command\": null`) for them. Omit them completely so that EVERY step in the playbook is 100% executable (either a valid API `tool_call` or a valid non-null CLI `command`).\n\n\n## OUTPUT JSON SCHEMA MANDATE\nCRITICAL FORMATTING MANDATE:\n- All keys and string values MUST be enclosed in standard DOUBLE QUOTES (\").\n- NEVER use Python single quotes (') for dictionary keys or strings.\n- Return raw, valid JSON only matching this schema:\n```json\n{{\n    \"reasoning_chain\": [\n        \"[Stage 1 Verification Gates]: Gate 1 (Function match) -> Gate 2 (Platform active) -> Gate 3 (Problem aligned) -> Gate 4 (Params complete) -> Result: tool_call | command\",\n        \"[Stage 2 Sequencing]: Ordered search/diagnostic steps before remediation\",\n        \"[Stage 3 Variables]: Formatted {{{{entities.var}}}} placeholders\",\n        \"[Stage 4 Fallbacks]: Mapped fallback actions\",\n        \"[Stage 5 Verification]: Added output parse instructions\",\n        \"[Stage 6 Recall]: Preserved 100% of diagnostic steps\",\n        \"[Stage 7 Branching]: Mapped execute_if conditions\",\n        \"[Stage 8 Timeouts]: Assigned timeout_seconds\",\n        \"[Stage 9 Confidence]: Evaluated conversion fidelity and confidence score\"\n    ],\n    \"sop_id\": \"SOP-{{file_name_slug}}-001\",\n    \"title\": \"Title of Playbook\",\n    \"platform\": \"primary platform (e.g. solandra, auth0, airretailer, mock, system)\",\n    \"category\": \"network | storage | system | logins | software | hardware\",\n    \"target_os\": \"windows | linux\",\n    \"description\": \"Comprehensive overview of what this playbook does\",\n    \"confidence_score\": 0.95,\n    \"confidence_level\": \"High\",\n    \"confidence_reason\": \"All actions mapped directly to registered platform tools with complete parameter schemas and high procedural fidelity.\",\n    \"required_entities\": [\"user_id\", \"email\", \"hostname\"],\n    \"trigger_words\": [\"unlock\", \"account\", \"login\", \"platform_name\"],\n    \"steps\": [\n        {{\n            \"step_number\": 1,\n            \"sop_step_description\": \"Detailed step objective\",\n            \"step_type\": \"tool_call | command\",\n            \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1-4 result and why tool_call/command was chosen; why this exact platform/function/command and comm_inputs/placeholders were used over alternatives.\",\n            \"platform\": \"platform name from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG (only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"function_name\": \"exact_function_name (from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG above, only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"comm_inputs\": {{\n                \"user_id\": \"{{{{entities.user_id}}}}\"\n            }},\n            \"command\": \"ipconfig /all (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"shell\": \"powershell (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"fallback_command\": null,\n            \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n            \"expected_output_contains\": \"unlocked\",\n            \"timeout_seconds\": 30,\n            \"output_variable\": \"step_result\",\n            \"output_parse_instruction\": \"Inspect output/JSON response and set status flags.\",\n            \"execute_if\": null,\n            \"is_dangerous\": false,\n            \"danger_warning\": null\n        }}\n    ]\n}}\n```\n\n## RAW SOP DOCUMENT TEXT TO TRANSLATE:\nReset a Locked-Out User's Password via CoreID Admin Console\nL1 Escalation Runbook — Solandra Corp IT Support\nsop_id\nSOL-PWD-RESET-L1-002\nresolution_type\nescalate_only\naudience\nL1 Support Agent (authorized admin)\nrequired_role\nCoreID: Password Reset Admin\norg\nSolandra Corp\napplication\nCoreID Admin Console (mock)\nPurpose\nWhen a user's self-service password reset fails or the user has no working verification channel, the ticket escalates to L1. An authorized L1 agent with the Password Reset Admin role in CoreID resets the password directly from the admin console.\nBecause the agent's own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and role-gated, this flow does not send a one-time verification code to the user. That code-based check exists to authenticate an otherwise-unverified self-service claimant; an admin acting under an authenticated, role-scoped session establishes identity through the caller checklist instead (Step 3).\nProcedure\nInputs\nOpen ticket with the requester's employee ID and manager name on file\nYour own authenticated CoreID session with the Password Reset Admin role assigned\nCaller identity verification checklist (minimum 2 of 4 items matched)\nSteps\nStep 1: Ticket reviewed\nNavigation: Ticketing system → open ticket → Requester Details panel\nConfirm the ticket category reads Password Reset — Escalated, and note the requester's employee ID and manager name. ⚠️ Do not proceed if the ticket is missing a manager name — request it from the requester first.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 1 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a computer screen displaying a ticket number and employee information.\n\n* The top-left corner has a dark blue bar with white text that reads \"CoreID\" and \"Admin Console\".\n* Below this, there is a light gray bar with white text that reads \"Dashboard\" and \"Tickets\".\n* In the center of the image, there is a white box with black text that reads \"Ticket #SOL-84213\" and \"Password Reset - Escalated\".\n* Below this, there are several lines of text that read \"Category: J. Employee\", \"Requester: SOL-04821\", \"Employee ID: A. Menon\", \"Manager: Finance\", and \"Opened: Today, 10:42 AM\".\n* On the left side of the image, there is a vertical menu bar with white text that reads \"Users\", \"Reports\", \"Settings\", and \"Settings\".\n* At the bottom of the image, there is a horizontal bar with white text that reads \"Signed in as: A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin)\".\n\nThe image appears to be a screenshot of a computer screen displaying a ticket number and employee information for a password reset request. The ticket is escalated and has been opened today at 10:42 AM. The employee ID is SOL-04821, and the manager is A. Menon. The category is J. Employee, and the requester is also SOL-04821. The image does not show any error messages or warnings. The likely issue category is Software/Application, and the severity is Medium. The observed facts are that the ticket is escalated and has been opened today at 10:42 AM. The inference is that the password reset request is being processed. The confidence score is 0.95, and the confidence level is High. The confidence reason is that the image is clear and the text is legible.\n\nStep 2: User located and lock confirmed\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → Users → Search bar\nSearch by employee ID. Confirm the account status badge shows Locked before continuing — do not reset a password on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 2 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.80 / Medium]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a user signed in as A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin). The user's name is SOL-04821, and they are an employee in the Finance department.\n\n* **CoreID Admin Console**\n\t+ The CoreID Admin Console is open on the left side of the screen.\n\t+ It has a dark blue background with white text.\n\t+ The menu options are: Admin Console, Dashboard, Tickets, Users, Reports, and Settings.\n* **User Information**\n\t+ The user's name is SOL-04821.\n\t+ They are an employee in the Finance department.\n* **Locked Button**\n\t+ There is a red button that says \"Locked\" in the bottom-right corner of the screen.\n\t+ It is likely that this button is used to lock or unlock the user's account.\n\n**Summary**\n\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a user signed in as A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin). The user's name is SOL-04821, and they are an employee in the Finance department. There is a red button that says \"Locked\" in the bottom-right corner of the screen.\n\n**Detailed Description**\n\n* The CoreID Admin Console is open on the left side of the screen.\n* It has a dark blue background with white text.\n* The menu options are: Admin Console, Dashboard, Tickets, Users, Reports, and Settings.\n* The user's name is SOL-04821.\n* They are an employee in the Finance department.\n* There is a red button that says \"Locked\" in the bottom-right corner of the screen.\n\n**Extracted Text**\n\n* None\n\n**Errors and Warnings**\n\n* None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity**\n\n* Category: Unknown\n* Severity: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference**\n\n* OBSERVED FACTS:\n\t+ The CoreID Admin Console is open on the left side of the screen.\n\t+ The user's name is SOL-04821.\n\t+ They are an employee in the Finance department.\n\t+ There is a red button that says \"Locked\" in the bottom-right corner of the screen.\n* INFERENCE:\n\t+ The user may have been locked out of their account.\n\t+ The reason for the lockout is unknown.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes**\n\n* CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.8\n* CONFIDENCE LEVEL: Medium\n* CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear, but the context is limited, making it difficult to determine the exact issue.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a white box in the center displaying a list of options for identity verification. The title \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\" is at the top of the box.\n\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is displayed on the left side of the image.\n    *   It has a dark blue background with white text.\n    *   The menu options are listed vertically, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n*   **White Box**\n    *   The white box is centered in the image and contains a list of options for identity verification.\n    *   The title \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\" is at the top of the box.\n    *   There are two checkboxes below the title, one for \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and one for \"Manager name matches ticket\".\n    *   Below the checkboxes, there is a dropdown menu with the option \"Department matches record\".\n    *   At the bottom of the box, there is a checkbox for \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\".\n    *   Below the checkbox, there is a message that reads \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n*   **Background**\n    *   The background of the image is a light gray color.\n\nIn summary, the image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console with a white box displaying a list of options for identity verification. The user has checked the boxes for \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and \"Manager name matches ticket\", and has selected the option \"Department matches record\" from the dropdown menu. The user has also checked the box for \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\". The message at the bottom of the box indicates that two out of four requirements have been met, and the minimum required to proceed is two.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a password reset page for an employee named J. Employee, with a temporary password provided and a button to generate a new one.\n\n* **CoreID Admin Console**\n\t+ The top-left corner of the image features a dark blue navigation bar with white text that reads \"CoreID\" and \"Admin Console\".\n\t+ Below this, there are several menu options, including \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n* **Reset Password - J. Employee**\n\t+ The main content area of the page is a white box with a title that reads \"Reset Password - J. Employee\" in black text.\n\t+ Below the title, there is a temporary password displayed in black text, which is \"Tq7#mVr2IkLp9x\".\n\t+ A checkbox labeled \"Force change at next sign-in\" is also present, but it is not selected.\n\t+ A blue button with white text that reads \"Generate Temporary Password\" is located below the checkbox.\n* **Generate Temporary Password**\n\t+ The button is centered in the white box and has a rounded rectangle shape.\n\t+ When clicked, it will generate a new temporary password for the user.\n* **Users**\n\t+ The \"Users\" menu option is highlighted in the navigation bar, indicating that the user is currently viewing the user management section of the CoreID Admin Console.\n* **Reports**\n\t+ The \"Reports\" menu option is also present in the navigation bar, but it is not highlighted.\n\t+ This suggests that the user may have access to reporting features within the CoreID Admin Console.\n* **Settings**\n\t+ The \"Settings\" menu option is located at the far right of the navigation bar.\n\t+ This option likely allows the user to customize various settings within the CoreID Admin Console.\n\nIn summary, the image shows a password reset page for an employee named J. Employee, with a temporary password provided and a button to generate a new one. The user is currently viewing the user management section of the CoreID Admin Console, and has access to reporting features and settings.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **Ticket Information**\n    *   Ticket number: SOL-84213\n    *   Status: Resolved\n    *   Resolution notes: Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is open, displaying the ticket resolution page.\n    *   The console has a dark blue background with white text and a light gray header.\n    *   The header includes the CoreID logo, the user's name (A. Rao), and the role (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin).\n*   **Ticket Resolution Page**\n    *   The ticket resolution page is displayed in the center of the screen.\n    *   The page has a white background with black text.\n    *   The page includes the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n    *   The resolution notes indicate that the identity was verified, a temporary password was issued, and the force-change-at-next-sign-in feature was enabled.\n    *   The page also mentions that no OTP was sent and that the admin-mediated reset was performed per policy.\n\nIn summary, the image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes, which indicate that the identity was verified, a temporary password was issued, and the force-change-at-next-sign-in feature was enabled. The page also mentions that no OTP was sent and that the admin-mediated reset was performed per policy.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## LINKED REFERENCE KNOWLEDGE (Scraped from embedded SOP URLs):\n### Reference Web Page [https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords]:\nURL: https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords\nStatus Code: 200\n\n--- SCRAPED MARKDOWN CONTENT START ---\n[Skip to main content](#main)\n\n![U.S. flag](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/us_flag_small.png)\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere’s how you know\n\nHere’s how you know\n\n![Dot gov](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-dot-gov.svg)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\n\n A **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n![HTTPS](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-https.svg)\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\n\n A **lock** (LockA locked padlock) or **https://** means you’ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[Staying Secure at Events](/staying-secure-large-scale-events)[no-cost Cyber Services](/resources-tools/resources/free-cybersecurity-services-and-tools \"Free Cyber Services\")[secure your business](/secureyourbusiness)[Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog](/kev)[Report A Cyber Issue](/report)\n\n[![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Security Defense Agency National Coordinator For Critical Infrastructure Security and Resilience](/sites/default/files/images/SVG/header_logo_tagline_update.svg)![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Security Defense Agency National Coordinator For Critical Infrastructure Secur\n\n## PREVIOUS ATTEMPT ERRORS (MUST FIX ON THIS RETRY):\n- Scenario 3/4 Validation Error (Attempt 1): [\"Layer 1 Error: Step 1 has step_type='tool_call' but 'platform' is missing.\"]\n\nAI: [{'name': 'get_platform_tools', 'args': {'platform_name': 'solandra'}, 'id': '7SpfWrPMP', 'type': 'tool_call'}]\nTool: {\n  \"platform\": \"solandra\",\n  \"aliases\": [\n    \"coreid\",\n    \"coreid admin console\",\n    \"coreid_user_management\",\n    \"solandra group\",\n    \"solandra corp\",\n    \"solandra_ticketing\",\n    \"solandra_group\",\n    \"ticketing system\",\n    \"ticketing\"\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Solandra Corp IT Support platform covering CoreID Admin Console user lookup, identity verification, temporary password generation, and ticket resolution.\",\n  \"functions\": [\n    {\n      \"function_name\": \"search_user\",\n      \"description\": \"Searches the CoreID user directory by employee ID and returns the matching user's account status and profile details.\",\n      \"required_inputs\": [\n        \"employee_id\"\n      ],\n      \"response_schema\": {\n        \"success\": {\n          \"found\": \"true\",\n          \"user_id\": \"str\",\n          \"employee_id\": \"str\",\n          \"name\": \"str\",\n          \"manager_name\": \"str\",\n          \"department\": \"str\",\n          \"status\": \"Locked\"\n        },\n        \"not_found\": {\n          \"found\": \"false\",\n          \"user_id\": \"null\"\n        },\n        \"error\": {\n          \"error\